  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1121 PM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThe system for this weekend is still on track. We're starting to \nsee winds increase in the higher terrain, with gusts around 30 to \n50mph already. Northwest flow aloft will continue to strengthen \nthrough Friday afternoon, remaining strong through late Saturday. \nWind gusts above tree line will be around 50 to 70mph late Friday \nmorning through the early afternoon. The strongest winds will be \nconfined to the highest elevations, but wind gusts will still\nreach around 30 to 50mph in the northern foothills. The strong \nnorthwest winds are expected to move off of the Cheyenne Ridge and\ninto our northern counties in the early morning, producing gusts \naround 30 to 50mph in the northern plains. \n\nWhile snow could start in the mountains on Friday, the peak lifting \nfrom the upper level shortwave and left entrance of the upper jet\nwill hit early Saturday morning continuing into the early \nafternoon. Mountains snow will start to increase overnight \nFriday into Saturday, with the heaviest snow Saturday morning and\nearly afternoon. There will be two potential big impacts to \nmountain travel on Saturday. The first potential impact is \nblowing snow. Wind gusts will be around 50 to 60mph at times, \nwhich when combined with fresh snow on the ground and snow falling\naround 0.5 to 1 inch an hour at times, could cause serious \nvisibility issues. The second potential impact is snow squalls. \nStrong frontogenesis and some weak instability could be enough for\nsnow squalls to develop in the mountains. Snow squalls cause \nrapid changes to visibilities and driving conditions, creating \nvery hazardous travel. QPF amounts continue to trend higher for \nour higher elevations. This combined with the strong winds leading\nto blowing snow and the holiday weekend travel has led us to \nupgrade our Park Range zone to a Winter Storm Warning. As of right\nnow, we've left our Front Range zones in a Winter Weather \nAdvisory, due to the lower snow totals, but travel conditions \ncould still be very hazardous if snow banding or snow squalls \ndevelop. \n\nThe timing of the cold front Saturday is still uncertain. A few \nof the high res models are trending faster, with the front \nentering our northern counties between 7 and 8am, reaching the \nmetro around 9, and exiting our southern counties by 11am (which \nis before the GFS even brings it into our northern counties). If \nthis happens, it would give the plains a much quicker transition \nfrom rain to snow and colder afternoon temperatures. We are \nleaning towards the quicker front solutions and have adjusted the \nforecast accordingly. A trace to a few tenths of snow still looks\npossible for the metro Saturday afternoon, but it will depend on\nthat front timing. the south metro, Palmer, and southern \nfoothills will be favored for better precip chances and snow \ntotals given the north/northeast flow behind the front. We are\nalso monitoring the potential for snow bands to develop due to \nthe strong frontogenesis. Areas that see a heavier snow bands \nwill be more likely for brief snow accumulations on roads and \npotential travel impacts. \n\nSnow chances will decrease Saturday evening as the shortwave and\njet move out of our area. Dry conditions will prevail Sunday \nthrough Tuesday for much of the area, with warmer temperature for \nthe start of next week. We could see a weak upper level \ndisturbance bring brief snow chances to the mountains on Monday, \nbut little to no impacts are expected. Models are showing an upper\nlevel low developing off the southwest coast on Tuesday, moving \neast over the four corners and then near our area. The exact \nprogression of the low is still uncertain in both timing and \nlocation, but this will be our next chance for both mountain and \nplains snowfall. Right now, the best chance for precip with this \nsystem will be late Wednesday into Thursday. Temperatures are \nforecast to drop back closer to normal behind the cold front \nassociated with this system.",
